   "text": {
    "L1": "A curved line cannot be measured directly with a straight scale. But its length can still be measured.",
    "L2": "A curved path between two points is longer than the straight line between them.",
    "L3": "Use a thread that does not stretch. Lay it carefully from the start along every bend to the end.",
    "L4": "Mark the thread where the curve ends. Then straighten the thread beside a scale and read the marked length.",
    "L5": "A flexible tape can wrap around a bottle, a waist or a tree trunk. A snug thread can work too.",
    "L6": "On a map, measure a winding route with thread. Multiply its centimetres by the map scale to find the real distance.",
    "L7": "The thread must follow the curve closely and have no gaps. Stretchy or loose material can give an unreliable reading.",
    "L8": "Now put the measuring steps in order."
   },
